WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.589 --> 00:00:07.940
Gary W. Goldstein: There is a wonderful quote…it’s
escaping me for a moment from a great science

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:15.099
fiction writer I believe it was who said “You
can’t allow your manuscripts to gather dust

00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:17.350
in a dresser drawer…”

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:22.160
Film Courage: Let’s talk about marketing
a screenplay.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.260
When does it happen?

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.490
How important is it?

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.050
How does a screenwriter do it effectively?

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:29.960
Gary W. Goldstein: It’s not important…it’s
essential.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:36.250
It’s crucial, it is life-affirming and it
is absolutely the responsibility of any talent,

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:37.590
any creative to market themselves.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:45.690
If it’s a writer and it’s a script or
a book or some product…time to market it!

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.920
That’s what you’ve got to do.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:55.520
And here is what most people don’t understand,
it should be and it is, fun.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.199
If I take that same writer to a cocktail party
and I put a drink in their hand and say “Tell

00:01:00.199 --> 00:01:02.710
these people, they are fascinated by you and
what you do, talk about it.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:04.610
They’re going to talk non-stop.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.109
You will not be able to shut them up.

00:01:07.109 --> 00:01:11.119
But if you, on the other hand say, “Ahh…you’ve
got a script.”

00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:12.600
and you’re at home.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.100
Pick up the phone and call someone.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.100
No.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.459
They don’t want to do it.

00:01:17.459 --> 00:01:19.590
Suddenly marketing is a negative.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:21.310
It’s a bad thing.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.110
It’s all in how you perceive it.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:28.479
Marketing and sharing your talent with the
world should be a joy.

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:32.200
It should be your purpose.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.240
The problem is, people thing so narrowly.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.729
99.9 percent of writers who have a screenplay
ready to go to market.

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:45.109
They’ve rewritten it, it’s ready, they’ve
tested it, beautiful.

00:01:45.109 --> 00:01:46.420
What do they do?

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:49.289
They automatically think “I need to send
it to agents.

00:01:49.289 --> 00:01:51.770
I need to send it to producers.”

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:52.930
Then they stop.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:53.939
The thought ends.

00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:56.079
Oh my gosh.

00:01:56.079 --> 00:02:01.819
That does not sound like a lot of fun because
who are the people getting all the scripts?

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:03.999
Producers and agents.

00:02:03.999 --> 00:02:06.799
I would look at it and say “Let me research
this.”

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:11.819
What are the kind of projects that are exciting
that have been produced successfully (projects

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:15.959
that people love) that are similar over the
last handful of years?

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:18.379
Whether it’s TV, film or whatever.

00:02:18.379 --> 00:02:20.629
And I’ll make a list and I’ll do a spreadsheet.

00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:24.610
And I’ll list the cinematographer and the
editor and the script supervisor and the casting

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.659
director and everybody but the producer and
the agent.

00:02:27.659 --> 00:02:31.319
And I will start calling them and reaching
out to their assistants.

00:02:31.319 --> 00:02:34.520
And saying “I’m calling because…Oh my
gosh…You guys were involved with one of

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.610
my favorite projects of all time.

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.530
I so admire the work.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:44.319
And I have something spiritually very similar.

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:46.860
I’d love your advice.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:48.810
Would you be my 5-minute mentor?

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.120
Would you play with me on this a little bit?”

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.790
There are endless conversations you can have.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:58.269
You develop a rapport with people because
you share a common interest.

00:02:58.269 --> 00:03:00.489
That’s the key!

00:03:00.489 --> 00:03:05.930
And if you can get them interested in a conversation
then you can get them soon on the third call

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:07.219
reading your script.

00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:10.760
You’re not asking anything of them.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.379
When you call an agent, you’re asking them
to represent you.

00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:15.900
That’s a huge ask!

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.610
When you send your script to a producer saying
“Will you produce this?”

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:19.660
Oh my God!

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.060
That’s the biggest ask of any.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:28.760
Because what that means is the producer has
to invest several years on their own dime

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:32.730
to create value and make this a reality.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:37.000
When you ask an agent to represent you or
a producer to produce your film, you have

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.409
to understand there is almost nothing in it
for them at the outset.

00:03:40.409 --> 00:03:48.079
They’re going to invest their time, their
belief and their relationships on your behalf

00:03:48.079 --> 00:03:49.400
for no money.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.900
That’s the reality.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.329
But if you make it about something else, if
you make it about a shared common interest

00:03:55.329 --> 00:04:02.569
that’s exciting and fun and those people
can become your ambassadors and backdoor that

00:04:02.569 --> 00:04:06.430
script over to that agent or that script over
to [another], it comes over in a different

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:07.430
package.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:09.080
It comes in a different context.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.319
That’s where magic happens.

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:15.430
That’s where alchemy kicks in.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:19.181
I could talk about that for about three days.

00:04:19.181 --> 00:04:19.200
Question for the Viewers: Are you excited
to share a script when it’s finished?

